  "query_preview": "..It was a very active day on Monday, with numerous\nthunderstorms, some severe with flash flooding. This continued\nwell into the night, especially in far northern Arizona where a\nlarge cluster of storms brought strong winds to the Page area\n(measured 58 mph) around midnight. Things have quieted down as of\n3 AM, with the last remaining cluster of rain over NW Coconino\nCounty where a widespread 0.50-1.00\" has fallen since midnight.\nWith such a large scale rainfall event, often the following day\nwill be more subdued with the rain-cooled air mass. However, an\nupper level disturbance currently located in SW New Mexico is\nforecast to move up into east central Arizona later today and then\ninto northern Arizona tonight. The latest model guidance does\nindicate a slower start to convection over much of our area today,\nbut with such deep moisture in place and an approaching\ndisturbance we still expect numerous storms to develop. The\ngreatest coverage by early to mid afternoon looks to be over\neastern Arizona, but these will spread westward and impact the\nrest of northern Arizona later in the afternoon and evening. The\nprimary threat continues to be heavy rainfall and flash flooding,\nespecially considering the wet ground conditions and many\nstreams/rivers are already flowing and elevated. For this reason,\nthere is a Flash Flood Watch in effect through late this evening\nfor all of our forecast area excluding the far northeast corner.\nThere is also a localized strong wind/hail threat today though\nwind shear is weaker than Monday. As the disturbance moves \nnorthward Wednesday, scattered thunderstorms are expected in the \nafternoon, especially over the higher terrain with a somewhat \nlower threat of heavy rainfall. Thursday into the weekend\n- the center of high pressure to our east is forecast to shift \ntoward Arizona. While moisture levels will drop, there will still \nbe enough for daily scattered storms over the higher terrain, but \nmore isolated for the valleys. Temperatures will also begin to \nheat up to above normal levels. Temperatures by the weekend may \napproach warning levels for the lowest elevations of the Grand \nCanyon so we'll keep an eye on that.",
